WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Practical SBOM work starts with clear scope.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:14.920
Define whether the SBOM describes source, a build environment, an executable, an installer, a package, a container, or another release artifact.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:23.980
Include source, binary, vendored, generated, build-time, runtime, and transitive components where they matter to that scope.

4
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.560
Clear scope keeps later review grounded.

5
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.320
Use standard machine-readable formats supported by the organization and its tools.

6
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:38.060
Generate SBOMs repeatably in the build or release workflow, not as a one-time manual spreadsheet.

7
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:47.680
Include useful component identity, versions, sources, licenses, hashes where practical, relationships, and enough build context to support real analysis.

8
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.600
Validate quality and watch for drift.

9
00:00:50.700 --> 00:01:01.180
Check for missing components, ambiguous names, unknown versions, duplicate records, incomplete relationships, and mismatches between the SBOM and the artifact.

10
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:10.000
Review major dependency changes, platform changes, and release packaging changes before the SBOM becomes part of the release record.

11
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.940
Small validation habits prevent large response delays later.

12
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.660
Finally, connect SBOMs to decisions.

13
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:26.400
Use them for vulnerability response, license review, supplier communication, customer requests, release confidence, and incident response.

14
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:33.140
Store and share SBOMs according to policy, assign owners, and keep them current.

15
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:39.480
An SBOM is a living engineering artifact that improves supply-chain visibility when the team maintains it.
